from pathlib import Path
from runpy import run_path
g=run_path(str(Path(__file__).with_name('author-tools-generator.py')))
L=g['lesson']; C=g['course']
def P(*a): return L(*a,language='python')

C('scikit-learn','Scikit-learn provides tools for small machine-learning tasks in Python. Learn features, fitting, predictions and fair checks using tiny data and built-in examples.','Use Python with scikit-learn installed in a separate environment. These lessons need no remote AI service or secret key. They do not run in the browser playground.','https://scikit-learn.org/stable/getting_started.html',[
P('Features and labels','A feature is an input measurement. A label is the known answer in a teaching example. Scikit-learn usually expects a table of feature rows.','X = [[1], [2], [8], [9]]\ny = [0, 0, 1, 1]\nprint(len(X), len(y))','The output is 4 4. Each one-value feature row has one matching class label. The labels are fictional groups.','Add one row and its matching label.','Do not give a different number of feature rows and labels.','What is y in this example?','Known class labels','The trained model'),
P('Fit a simple classifier','A classifier chooses a group label. fit lets the model learn from examples. A decision tree splits inputs using learned thresholds.','from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\nX = [[1], [2], [8], [9]]\ny = [0, 0, 1, 1]\nmodel = DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=1, random_state=0)\nmodel.fit(X, y)\nprint(model.predict([[1.5], [8.5]]).tolist())','The predictions are [0, 1]. The shallow tree separates the low and high numbers in this toy dataset.','Predict the class for 2.5.','A toy split does not prove a model will work on a real-world problem.','Which method learns from the examples?','fit','predict'),
P('Keep test data separate','A test set holds examples kept out of model fitting. It gives a better check than only asking about examples the model already saw.','from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nX = [[n] for n in range(10)]\ny = [0] * 5 + [1] * 5\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.2, random_state=0, stratify=y)\nprint(len(X_train), len(X_test))','The output is 8 2. Two examples are held out. stratify helps preserve the class proportions for this split.','Explain why test labels should not guide model fitting.','Repeatedly choosing changes based on test results can leak information from the test set.','How many examples are held out?','2','8'),
P('Use a simple baseline','A baseline is a simple result to compare against. DummyClassifier can always choose the most frequent training class.','from sklearn.dummy import DummyClassifier\nmodel = DummyClassifier(strategy="most_frequent")\nmodel.fit([[1], [2], [3]], [0, 0, 1])\nprint(model.predict([[8], [9]]).tolist())','The output is [0, 0]. This baseline ignores the feature values and chooses the common class.','Change the training labels so 1 is most common.','Beating no comparison at all is not strong evidence; start with a simple baseline.','What does this baseline always choose?','The most frequent training class','The largest input number'),
P('Measure accuracy','Accuracy is the fraction of labels predicted correctly. It can be misleading when one class is much more common than another.','from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score\nprint(accuracy_score([0, 1, 1, 0], [0, 1, 0, 0]))','The result is 0.75 because three of four predictions match.','Find which prediction is wrong by comparing positions.','Do not rely only on accuracy for rare but important outcomes.','How many predictions are correct?','3 of 4','4 of 4'),
P('Read a confusion matrix','A confusion matrix counts correct and incorrect class choices. With labels [0, 1], rows are true labels and columns are predicted labels.','from sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix\nprint(confusion_matrix([0, 0, 1, 1], [0, 1, 1, 1], labels=[0, 1]).tolist())','The result is [[1, 1], [0, 2]]. One true 0 was predicted as 1; both true 1 values were correct.','Point to the count of true 0 values predicted as 1.','Check row and column meanings before interpreting a matrix.','What do rows represent here?','True labels','Only guessed labels'),
P('Scale using training data','Scaling puts features on a chosen numeric scale. Fit the scaler on training data, then use that fitted scaler on other data.','from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nscaler = StandardScaler()\nscaled = scaler.fit_transform([[1.0], [2.0], [3.0]])\nprint(round(float(scaled[1, 0]), 6))','The middle value becomes 0.0 because it equals the training mean. Other values are measured relative to the learned spread.','Explain why the test set should not set the training mean.','Fitting the scaler on all data before splitting leaks test information.','Which data should fit the scaler?','Training data','The final test set'),
P('Pipelines keep steps together','A pipeline applies preparation and a model in the intended order. It helps use the same preparation for fitting and prediction.','from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\nmodel = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=1))\nmodel.fit([[1], [2], [8], [9]], [0, 0, 1, 1])\nprint(model.predict([[1.5], [8.5]]).tolist())','The result is [0, 1]. The pipeline scales the input and then asks the nearest-neighbour model for labels.','Name the first and second pipeline steps.','Do not manually apply a second, different scaling step to predictions.','What runs first in this pipeline?','StandardScaler','The label output'),
P('Predict a number with regression','Regression predicts a number instead of a class. LinearRegression fits a straight-line relationship to the examples.','from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nmodel = LinearRegression().fit([[1], [2], [3]], [2, 4, 6])\nprint(round(float(model.predict([[4]])[0]), 2))','The output is 8.0. The training examples follow the exact rule output = 2 times input.','Predict for input 5.','A real dataset may not follow a perfect straight line.','What does regression predict?','A number','Only a class name'),
P('Measure numeric error','Mean absolute error averages the size of prediction mistakes without keeping their plus or minus signs.','from sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error\nprint(mean_absolute_error([2, 4], [3, 4]))','The result is 0.5. The absolute errors are 1 and 0, whose average is one half.','Calculate the error for predictions [2, 6].','The error is in the target\'s units; do not compare unrelated tasks without context.','What is the mean absolute error here?','0.5','1.5'),
P('Cross-validation repeats a check','Cross-validation trains and evaluates on several train/validation splits. It gives more than one estimate, but still needs careful data grouping.','from sklearn.datasets import load_iris\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\nX, y = load_iris(return_X_y=True)\nscores = cross_val_score(DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=2, random_state=0), X, y, cv=3)\nprint(len(scores))','The output is 3 because three validation folds were used. Iris is included with scikit-learn; no download is needed.','Explain why three scores can differ from one another.','Related records from the same person or future time must not leak across splits.','How many validation scores are produced?','3','150'),
P('Inspect the model limit','A model setting chosen before fitting is a hyperparameter. max_depth limits how many splits a tree can make along a path.','from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\nmodel = DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=1, random_state=0)\nmodel.fit([[1], [2], [8], [9]], [0, 0, 1, 1])\nprint(model.get_depth())','The result is 1. The tree learned one split level for this dataset. A larger limit allows more complex rules.','Compare depth 1 with depth 2 using a separate validation set in a larger experiment.','More complex models can memorise training details instead of learning useful patterns.','What does max_depth limit?','Tree split depth','The number of computer screens'),
])

tf=[
('Tensors hold numbers','A tensor is a number container with a shape. It can be a single number, a list or a table.','x = tf.constant([2, 4, 6])\nprint(x.numpy().tolist())','The output is [2, 4, 6]. numpy exposes the eager tensor values; tolist makes the result easy to read.','Add one value to the tensor.','TensorFlow is a separate Python library; this website does not run it.','What does this tensor hold?','Three numbers','A trained language model'),
('Shape and rank','Shape lists dimension sizes. Rank is the number of dimensions, not a score of model quality.','x = tf.constant([[1, 2], [3, 4]])\nprint(x.shape, x.ndim)','The output shows shape (2, 2) and rank 2. This tensor is a two-dimensional table.','Draw a tensor with shape (2, 3).','Do not confuse tensor rank with matrix rank from linear algebra.','What is the rank here?','2','4'),
('Choose the numeric type','A dtype tells TensorFlow how to store tensor values. Many model computations use floating-point numbers.','x = tf.constant([1, 2], dtype=tf.float32)\nprint(x.dtype.name)','The output is float32. The explicit type controls the numeric representation.','Print x.numpy().tolist too.','Mixing incompatible numeric types can require an explicit cast.','Which dtype is selected?','float32','string'),
('Tensor arithmetic','Operators can work element by element on matching tensor shapes.','a = tf.constant([1, 2])\nb = tf.constant([3, 4])\nprint((a + b).numpy().tolist())','The output is [4, 6]. Matching positions are added.','Multiply matching positions instead.','Element-wise multiplication is different from matrix multiplication.','What does addition combine here?','Matching positions','Every possible pair'),
('Broadcast a scalar','A scalar is a single value. Broadcasting can apply it across all positions of a tensor.','x = tf.constant([2, 4, 6])\nprint((x * 2).numpy().tolist())','The output is [4, 8, 12]. Each value is multiplied by 2.','Add 3 to every position.','Broadcasting still follows shape rules for non-scalar tensors.','What is a scalar?','A single value','Only a large table'),
('Reduce to a summary','A reduction combines values into a summary, such as a sum or mean.','x = tf.constant([2.0, 4.0, 6.0])\nprint(float(tf.reduce_mean(x).numpy()))','The result is 4.0. The three values sum to 12 and there are three of them.','Find the sum with reduce_sum.','An average can hide variation in the input values.','What summary is calculated?','Mean','Maximum'),
('Reshape the same data','reshape changes the arrangement while preserving the number of values.','x = tf.constant([1, 2, 3, 4])\nprint(tf.reshape(x, (2, 2)).numpy().tolist())','The output is [[1, 2], [3, 4]]. Four numbers fit a two-by-two table.','Reshape to (1, 4).','The product of new dimension sizes must match the number of values.','Can four values fill a 2 by 3 tensor?','No','Yes'),
('Variables can change','A TensorFlow Variable holds a value that can be updated. Model weights are usually stored in variables.','count = tf.Variable(2.0)\ncount.assign_add(1.0)\nprint(float(count.numpy()))','The output is 3.0. assign_add changes the stored value by adding 1.','Assign a completely new value with assign.','A constant tensor and a mutable Variable have different purposes.','Which object is meant to hold changing values?','Variable','A fixed constant'),
('Gradients describe a local change','A gradient describes how an output changes near the current input. GradientTape records operations so TensorFlow can calculate a derivative.','x = tf.Variable(3.0)\nwith tf.GradientTape() as tape:\n    y = x * x\nprint(float(tape.gradient(y, x).numpy()))','The result is 6.0. For x squared, the derivative at x is 2 times x, so at 3 it is 6.','Try x equal to 2.','Record the calculation inside the tape context; it cannot infer unrecorded work.','What gradient is produced at x = 3?','6','9'),
('A dense layer combines inputs','A dense layer learns weights that combine input features. Here a layer with three units produces three values for each input row.','layer = tf.keras.layers.Dense(3)\nx = tf.constant([[1.0, 2.0]])\nprint(tuple(layer(x).shape))','The output shape is (1, 3): one row and three layer outputs. The initial output values depend on random weights.','Change the layer to two units and predict the shape.','An untrained layer\'s numbers are not useful predictions just because it runs.','How many outputs per row does Dense(3) make?','3','2'),
('Train a tiny model','Training adjusts weights to reduce a loss, which measures mistakes. This toy example fits numbers near a simple doubling rule.','import numpy as np\nx = np.array([[1.], [2.], [3.], [4.]], dtype="float32")\ny = np.array([[2.], [4.], [6.], [8.]], dtype="float32")\nmodel = tf.keras.Sequential([tf.keras.Input(shape=(1,)), tf.keras.layers.Dense(1)])\nmodel.compile(optimizer="sgd", loss="mse")\nmodel.fit(x, y, epochs=20, verbose=0)\nprint(tuple(model(x).shape))','The shape is (4, 1). mse means mean squared error. Twenty training passes do not guarantee exact doubling or real-world accuracy.','Separate a new input for checking instead of judging only the training examples.','Do not treat a tiny training demonstration as a validated model.','What does the loss measure?','Prediction mistakes','The number of folders'),
('Save an untrained model carefully','Keras can save a model to a .keras file. Saving preserves a model; it does not improve its accuracy.','model = tf.keras.Sequential([tf.keras.Input(shape=(1,)), tf.keras.layers.Dense(1)])\nmodel.save("practice-model.keras")','A model file is written in the current directory. This particular model is untrained, so it must not be described as an accurate predictor.','Choose a fresh filename in a practice folder.','Do not load model files from untrusted sources without understanding the format and risks.','Does saving a model train it?','No','Yes'),
]
C('tensorflow','TensorFlow calculates with tensors and trains models. Learn small numeric operations, gradients and a first Keras model while keeping training demonstrations separate from proven results.','Examples need Python and TensorFlow in a separate local environment. Some installations are large; follow the official supported-platform guide. No paid API or cloud account is needed.','https://www.tensorflow.org/guide/basics',[P(t,i,'import tensorflow as tf\n\n'+c,r,p,w,q,a,b) for t,i,c,r,p,w,q,a,b in tf])

torch=[
('Create a tensor','A PyTorch tensor stores numbers with a shape and a data type. torch.tensor makes one from a small Python list.','x = torch.tensor([2, 4, 6])\nprint(x.tolist())','The output is [2, 4, 6]. tolist returns ordinary Python values for easy inspection.','Add 8 to the list.','A tensor is a data container, not a trained model by itself.','What does this tensor contain?','Three numbers','Three trained models'),
('Inspect shape and dimensions','Shape gives the size of each dimension. ndim tells you how many dimensions there are.','x = torch.tensor([[1, 2], [3, 4]])\nprint(tuple(x.shape), x.ndim)','The output is (2, 2) 2. The tensor is a two-by-two table with two dimensions.','Draw a three-row one-column tensor.','Two dimensions do not mean only two total values.','How many total values are here?','4','2'),
('Set a dtype','A dtype specifies how numbers are represented. Floating-point tensors are commonly used for model calculations and gradients.','x = torch.tensor([1, 2], dtype=torch.float32)\nprint(x.dtype)','The output is torch.float32. Both values use that type.','Read x.tolist to see 1.0 and 2.0.','Integer tensors cannot be used for every gradient calculation.','Which dtype is requested?','float32','An arbitrary text type'),
('Add matching values','Tensor arithmetic can combine matching positions. Shapes must match or follow broadcasting rules.','a = torch.tensor([1, 2])\nb = torch.tensor([3, 4])\nprint((a + b).tolist())','The output is [4, 6]. The first values add together and so do the second values.','Subtract b from a.','Adding tensors is different from joining two Python lists.','What is the first output value?','4','1'),
('Use matrix multiplication','Matrix multiplication combines rows with columns. The inner dimensions must match. It is different from element-wise multiplication.','a = torch.tensor([[1., 2.]])\nb = torch.tensor([[3.], [4.]])\nprint((a @ b).item())','The result is 11.0 because 1 times 3 plus 2 times 4 equals 11. @ performs matrix multiplication.','Change the final 4 to 5 and calculate by hand.','Use * for element-wise multiplication and @ for matrix multiplication.','What does @ mean here?','Matrix multiplication','Text joining'),
('Reshape values','reshape changes the layout of a tensor while keeping the same number of values.','x = torch.tensor([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6])\nprint(x.reshape(2, 3).tolist())','The output is [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]. Six values fit two rows of three.','Try shape (3, 2).','A new shape must contain the same total number of values.','Which shape can hold six values?','2 by 3','2 by 2'),
('Track a gradient','requires_grad asks PyTorch to track operations for automatic differentiation. backward fills the gradient of the scalar result with respect to the input.','x = torch.tensor(3.0, requires_grad=True)\ny = x * x\ny.backward()\nprint(x.grad.item())','The result is 6.0. The local slope of x squared at 3 is twice 3.','Repeat in a fresh tensor with value 2.','Gradients accumulate by default; clear them between training steps.','What does x.grad hold?','The computed gradient','The original value squared'),
('Disable tracking for a calculation','torch.no_grad runs operations without building a gradient-tracking graph. This is useful when you only need predictions.','x = torch.tensor(3.0, requires_grad=True)\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    y = x * 2\nprint(y.requires_grad)','The result is False. The output is not tracked for a backward calculation.','Print y.item to see its value too.','no_grad and model.eval do different jobs; one does not replace the other.','What does no_grad disable here?','Gradient tracking','All tensor calculation'),
('A linear layer learns weights','nn.Linear combines input features into output values using learned weights and a bias. Two inputs can produce one output.','layer = torch.nn.Linear(2, 1)\nx = torch.tensor([[1.0, 2.0]])\nprint(tuple(layer(x).shape))','The output shape is (1, 1). Its numeric value depends on initial random weights.','Change the output size to 3 and predict the shape.','Running an untrained layer does not make its predictions meaningful.','What does Linear(2, 1) expect per row?','2 input features','1 input feature'),
('Build a small sequential model','A sequential model applies layers in order. An activation such as ReLU keeps positive values and changes negative values to zero.','model = torch.nn.Sequential(torch.nn.Linear(2, 3), torch.nn.ReLU(), torch.nn.Linear(3, 1))\nprint(tuple(model(torch.tensor([[1.0, 2.0]])).shape))','The model produces shape (1, 1). The middle layer lets the model represent more than one plain linear transformation.','Name the input and output size of each linear layer.','Adjacent layers must have compatible sizes.','What does ReLU do to negative values?','Changes them to zero','Always doubles them'),
('One training step','A training step clears old gradients, computes loss, finds new gradients and updates weights. Each step has a separate job.','model = torch.nn.Linear(1, 1)\noptimizer = torch.optim.SGD(model.parameters(), lr=0.01)\nx = torch.tensor([[1.], [2.]])\ny = torch.tensor([[2.], [4.]])\noptimizer.zero_grad()\nloss = torch.nn.functional.mse_loss(model(x), y)\nloss.backward()\noptimizer.step()\nprint(loss.item() >= 0)','The check prints True because squared-error loss is nonnegative. One update is only a demonstration, not a trained reliable model.','Point to the line that changes the weights.','Forgetting zero_grad can mix gradients from multiple steps unintentionally.','Which line updates weights?','optimizer.step()','optimizer.zero_grad()'),
('Evaluation mode and checking','model.eval switches training-sensitive layers to evaluation behaviour. Use it with no_grad when making ordinary evaluation predictions.','model = torch.nn.Sequential(torch.nn.Linear(1, 1))\nmodel.eval()\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    result = model(torch.tensor([[3.0]]))\nprint(tuple(result.shape))','The shape is (1, 1). This model is untrained; the example shows the evaluation procedure, not an accurate prediction.','Explain why held-out examples are needed to judge usefulness.','eval does not train a model and does not by itself disable gradients.','Does eval disable gradient tracking by itself?','No','Yes'),
]
C('pytorch','PyTorch works with tensors and neural-network layers. Learn shapes, simple calculations, gradients and training steps using small examples with honest limits.','Run these examples only in a separate Python environment with PyTorch installed. A CPU setup is enough for the tiny examples; no hosted model or secret key is required.','https://pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html',[P(t,i,'import torch\n\n'+c,r,p,w,q,a,b) for t,i,c,r,p,w,q,a,b in torch])
